+### Interactive two-factor or SSO sign-in: attach to the user's Chrome
+
+Prefer the bundled headless browser, with saved auth state where the site has
+it (`state-load`). Attached Chrome runs in front of the user, so it suits
+supervised tasks rather than autonomous QA.
+
+The bundled browser is Chrome for Testing, which cannot complete an interactive
+two-factor or single sign-on step. When a task needs such a sign-in and no saved
+state exists, go straight to the user's everyday Chrome rather than attempting
+it in the bundled browser. Ask the user to sign in there, then attach:
+
+```bash
+node "$SKILL_DIR/scripts/pw.js" attach --extension=chrome
+```
+
+Run `attach` before asking about Playwright's Chrome extension (it shows as
+"Playwright Extension"): it is often already installed and approved, and the
+command connects at once. Ask the user to install it only if `attach` cannot
+connect. Otherwise the command waits until the user approves the connection in
+Chrome and picks a tab, so run it in the background or with a long timeout.
+Pass the session name it prints to later commands (`-s=<session>`).
+
+You are now acting in the user's real tab with their account: navigation and
+clicks happen in front of them, so change nothing they have not asked for. Run
+commands from the artifact directory, because the CLI writes page snapshots to
+`.playwright-cli/` in the current directory and they can hold the signed-in
+account's data. Finish with `detach`.
+
+The attached tab is usually in the background while the user works in another
+one, and clicks there time out waiting for the element to become stable. Bring
+it to the front before interacting:
+
+```bash
+node "$SKILL_DIR/scripts/pw.js" run-code 'async (page) => { await page.bringToFront(); }'
+```
+
+To replace text in a code editor on the page (CodeMirror, Monaco), type it: click
+into the editor, press `Meta+A` and `Backspace`, then call
+`page.keyboard.insertText(...)` in `run-code`. Setting the value through the
+editor's own API changes what the editor shows but not the form state the page
+submits, so the page saves its previous content. Read the saved value back after
+a reload.
+
